## pengu
## Layer-2 wrapper over the 28 input and gesture functions of the `raylib` binding that
## cross a boundary worth crossing.
##
## The binding flattens **every** enum parameter to `int`. Restoring the real types is the
## whole point of this family:
##
##     calling raylib.IsKeyDown with (raylib.KEY_SPACE to int)   # binding
##     calling is_key_down with raylib.KEY_SPACE   # wrapper
##
## The other half of the family is the failure sentinels: `key_pressed`, `char_pressed`,
## `gamepad_button_pressed`, `touch_point_id`, `detected_gesture` and `key_name` all
## report "nothing happened" through a magic value, so they return `maybe`.
##
## What is deliberately **absent** is as important: a zero-argument getter that cannot fail
## (`GetMousePosition`, `GetMouseDelta`, `GetTouchX`, `GetGestureDragVector`, ...) is not
## wrapped, because the binding call is already clear and a wrapper would only add a jump
## (contract section 9). Read those through `raylib` directly.
##
## This is family `input` of `raylibx`; see `docs/plans/md`.
##
## ## No window needed
## Every wrapped function is safe to call headless: with no input the predicates are
## `false`, the sentinel wrappers are `maybe none` and the setters simply record state.
## That is why this family has real CI tests rather than a compile-only probe.
##
## ## Memory
## No allocation and nothing to release. `std.ffi` appears only in `key_name` and
## `gamepad_name`, which copy a raylib-owned `const char*` and free nothing.
##
## ## Example
## ```pengu
## import raylib
## import raylibx_input
##
## weave main into int:
##     var jump as bool is calling is_key_pressed with raylib.KEY_SPACE
##     var typed as maybe int is calling key_pressed
##     var name as string is calling gamepad_name with 0
##     return 0
## ```


import raylib
import std.ffi
import std.ward


## Reports whether a key went down during this frame.
##
## ## Parameters
## - `key`: the key to test, as a `raylib.KeyboardKey`.
##
## ## Returns
## `true` when the condition holds for this frame, `false` otherwise.
##
## ## Notes
## This is the edge-triggered form: it is true only on the frame the key is first pressed.
##
## ## Memory
## No allocation; a single direct C call.
##
## ## Example
## ```pengu
## var down as bool is calling is_key_pressed with raylib.KEY_SPACE
## ```

weave is_key_pressed with key as raylib.KeyboardKey into bool:
    return calling raylib.IsKeyPressed with (key to int)

## Reports whether a key is auto-repeating this frame.
##
## ## Parameters
## - `key`: the key to test, as a `raylib.KeyboardKey`.
##
## ## Returns
## `true` when the condition holds for this frame, `false` otherwise.
##
## ## Notes
## True while the operating system keeps generating repeat events for a held key.
##
## ## Memory
## No allocation; a single direct C call.
##
## ## Example
## ```pengu
## var down as bool is calling is_key_pressed_repeat with raylib.KEY_SPACE
## ```

weave is_key_pressed_repeat with key as raylib.KeyboardKey into bool:
    return calling raylib.IsKeyPressedRepeat with (key to int)

## Reports whether a key is currently held.
##
## ## Parameters
## - `key`: the key to test, as a `raylib.KeyboardKey`.
##
## ## Returns
## `true` when the condition holds for this frame, `false` otherwise.
##
## ## Notes
## This is the level-triggered form: it stays true for as long as the key is held.
##
## ## Memory
## No allocation; a single direct C call.
##
## ## Example
## ```pengu
## var down as bool is calling is_key_down with raylib.KEY_SPACE
## ```

weave is_key_down with key as raylib.KeyboardKey into bool:
    return calling raylib.IsKeyDown with (key to int)

## Reports whether a key went up during this frame.
##
## ## Parameters
## - `key`: the key to test, as a `raylib.KeyboardKey`.
##
## ## Returns
## `true` when the condition holds for this frame, `false` otherwise.
##
## ## Memory
## No allocation; a single direct C call.
##
## ## Example
## ```pengu
## var down as bool is calling is_key_released with raylib.KEY_SPACE
## ```

weave is_key_released with key as raylib.KeyboardKey into bool:
    return calling raylib.IsKeyReleased with (key to int)

## Reports whether a key is currently not held.
##
## ## Parameters
## - `key`: the key to test, as a `raylib.KeyboardKey`.
##
## ## Returns
## `true` when the condition holds for this frame, `false` otherwise.
##
## ## Memory
## No allocation; a single direct C call.
##
## ## Example
## ```pengu
## var down as bool is calling is_key_up with raylib.KEY_SPACE
## ```

weave is_key_up with key as raylib.KeyboardKey into bool:
    return calling raylib.IsKeyUp with (key to int)

## Returns the next key from the input queue, consuming it.
##
## ## Returns
## `some` with the key's ordinal, or `maybe none` when the queue is empty.
##
## ## Notes
## The value is an `int` because raylib signals "nothing" with `0`, which is also `KEY_NULL`; the compiler cannot convert an `int` to a qualified omen (it emits an invalid C cast), so compare it as `k == (raylib.KEY_A to int)`, which is verified to work.
## Call it in a loop until it returns `maybe none` to drain the queue.
##
## ## Memory
## No allocation; a single direct C call.
##
## ## Example
## ```pengu
## var k as maybe int is calling key_pressed
## ```

weave key_pressed into maybe int:
    var k as int is calling raylib.GetKeyPressed
    if k == 0:
        return maybe none
    return some k

## Returns the next character from the input queue, consuming it.
##
## ## Returns
## `some` with the character's codepoint, or `maybe none` when the queue is empty.
##
## ## Notes
## The value is a Unicode codepoint, not a byte.
## Call it in a loop until it returns `maybe none` to drain the queue.
##
## ## Memory
## No allocation; a single direct C call.
##
## ## Example
## ```pengu
## var c as maybe int is calling char_pressed
## ```

weave char_pressed into maybe int:
    var c as int is calling raylib.GetCharPressed
    if c == 0:
        return maybe none
    return some c

## Returns the name of a key on the current keyboard layout.
##
## ## Parameters
## - `key`: the key to look up.
##
## ## Returns
## `some` with the name, or `maybe none` when raylib returns nothing.
##
## ## Notes
## **Measured: without a window this returns `NULL` for every key**, including `KEY_A`, because the keyboard layout is not available yet. The wrapper copies the string when there is one and frees nothing.
##
## ## Memory
## No allocation; a single direct C call.
##
## ## Example
## ```pengu
## var name as maybe string is calling key_name with raylib.KEY_A
## ```

weave key_name with key as raylib.KeyboardKey into maybe string:
    let c_name as ref to frozen char is calling raylib.GetKeyName with (key to int)
    if calling ffi.is_null with (transmute c_name to ref to void):
        return maybe none
    return some (calling ffi.string_from_cstr with (transmute c_name to ref to char))

## Reports whether a gamepad button went down during this frame.
##
## ## Parameters
## - `gamepad`: the gamepad slot, `0` to `3`.
## - `button`: the button to test, as a `raylib.GamepadButton`.
##
## ## Returns
## `true` when the condition holds for this frame, `false` otherwise. An absent gamepad reports `false`.
##
## ## Notes
## The gamepad slot is a plain `int` in raylib, not an enum.
##
## ## Memory
## No allocation; a single direct C call.
##
## ## Example
## ```pengu
## var down as bool is calling is_gamepad_button_pressed with 0, raylib.GAMEPAD_BUTTON_RIGHT_FACE_DOWN
## ```

weave is_gamepad_button_pressed with gamepad as int, button as raylib.GamepadButton into bool:
    return calling raylib.IsGamepadButtonPressed with gamepad, (button to int)

## Reports whether a gamepad button is currently held.
##
## ## Parameters
## - `gamepad`: the gamepad slot, `0` to `3`.
## - `button`: the button to test, as a `raylib.GamepadButton`.
##
## ## Returns
## `true` when the condition holds for this frame, `false` otherwise. An absent gamepad reports `false`.
##
## ## Notes
## The gamepad slot is a plain `int` in raylib, not an enum.
##
## ## Memory
## No allocation; a single direct C call.
##
## ## Example
## ```pengu
## var down as bool is calling is_gamepad_button_down with 0, raylib.GAMEPAD_BUTTON_RIGHT_FACE_DOWN
## ```

weave is_gamepad_button_down with gamepad as int, button as raylib.GamepadButton into bool:
    return calling raylib.IsGamepadButtonDown with gamepad, (button to int)

## Reports whether a gamepad button went up during this frame.
##
## ## Parameters
## - `gamepad`: the gamepad slot, `0` to `3`.
## - `button`: the button to test, as a `raylib.GamepadButton`.
##
## ## Returns
## `true` when the condition holds for this frame, `false` otherwise. An absent gamepad reports `false`.
##
## ## Notes
## The gamepad slot is a plain `int` in raylib, not an enum.
##
## ## Memory
## No allocation; a single direct C call.
##
## ## Example
## ```pengu
## var down as bool is calling is_gamepad_button_released with 0, raylib.GAMEPAD_BUTTON_RIGHT_FACE_DOWN
## ```

weave is_gamepad_button_released with gamepad as int, button as raylib.GamepadButton into bool:
    return calling raylib.IsGamepadButtonReleased with gamepad, (button to int)

## Reports whether a gamepad button is currently not held.
##
## ## Parameters
## - `gamepad`: the gamepad slot, `0` to `3`.
## - `button`: the button to test, as a `raylib.GamepadButton`.
##
## ## Returns
## `true` when the condition holds for this frame, `false` otherwise. An absent gamepad reports `false`.
##
## ## Notes
## The gamepad slot is a plain `int` in raylib, not an enum.
##
## ## Memory
## No allocation; a single direct C call.
##
## ## Example
## ```pengu
## var down as bool is calling is_gamepad_button_up with 0, raylib.GAMEPAD_BUTTON_RIGHT_FACE_DOWN
## ```

weave is_gamepad_button_up with gamepad as int, button as raylib.GamepadButton into bool:
    return calling raylib.IsGamepadButtonUp with gamepad, (button to int)

## Returns the last gamepad button pressed.
##
## ## Returns
## `some` with the button's ordinal, or `maybe none` when no button was pressed.
##
## ## Notes
## raylib signals "nothing" with `GAMEPAD_BUTTON_UNKNOWN`, which is **`0`**, not `-1` (measured: `GetGamepadButtonPressed()` returns `0` headless and the enum says `GAMEPAD_BUTTON_UNKNOWN is 0`).
## The value is an `int` for the same reason as `key_pressed`; compare it as `b == (raylib.GAMEPAD_BUTTON_RIGHT_FACE_DOWN to int)`.
##
## ## Memory
## No allocation; a single direct C call.
##
## ## Example
## ```pengu
## var b as maybe int is calling gamepad_button_pressed
## ```

weave gamepad_button_pressed into maybe int:
    var b as int is calling raylib.GetGamepadButtonPressed
    if b <= 0:
        return maybe none
    return some b

## Returns how far a gamepad axis is pushed.
##
## ## Parameters
## - `gamepad`: the gamepad slot, `0` to `3`.
## - `axis`: the axis to read, as a `raylib.GamepadAxis`.
##
## ## Returns
## The axis value, normally `[-1..1]`; `0.0` for an absent gamepad or a neutral stick.
##
## ## Notes
## A dead zone is the caller's business: raylib reports the raw value.
##
## ## Memory
## No allocation; a single direct C call.
##
## ## Example
## ```pengu
## var x as f32 is calling gamepad_axis_movement with 0, raylib.GAMEPAD_AXIS_LEFT_X
## ```

weave gamepad_axis_movement with gamepad as int, axis as raylib.GamepadAxis into f32:
    return calling raylib.GetGamepadAxisMovement with gamepad, (axis to int)

## Returns the name of a connected gamepad.
##
## ## Parameters
## - `gamepad`: the gamepad slot, `0` to `3`.
##
## ## Returns
## The gamepad name, or an **empty string** when the slot is empty.
##
## ## Notes
## Measured: raylib returns a non-null empty string for an absent gamepad, never `NULL`, so this is **not** a `maybe`.
##
## ## Memory
## No allocation; a single direct C call.
##
## ## Example
## ```pengu
## var who as string is calling gamepad_name with 0
## ```

weave gamepad_name with gamepad as int into string:
    let c_name as ref to frozen char is calling raylib.GetGamepadName with gamepad
    return calling ffi.string_from_cstr with (transmute c_name to ref to char)

## Loads gamepad mappings from a string.
##
## ## Parameters
## - `mappings`: mapping definitions in raylib's gamepad mapping format.
##
## ## Returns
## The number of mappings added, as raylib reports it; `0` when nothing was added.
##
## ## Notes
## raylib does not document a failure value, so this is a plain `int` rather than a `maybe` or a `bool`.
##
## ## Memory
## No allocation; a single direct C call.
##
## ## Example
## ```pengu
## var added as int is calling set_gamepad_mappings with mapping_text
## ```

weave set_gamepad_mappings with mappings as string into int:
    let c_mappings as ref to frozen char is calling ffi.cstr_from_string with mappings
    return calling raylib.SetGamepadMappings with c_mappings

## Reports whether a mouse button went down during this frame.
##
## ## Parameters
## - `button`: the button to test, as a `raylib.MouseButton`.
##
## ## Returns
## `true` when the condition holds for this frame, `false` otherwise.
##
## ## Memory
## No allocation; a single direct C call.
##
## ## Example
## ```pengu
## var down as bool is calling is_mouse_button_pressed with raylib.MOUSE_BUTTON_LEFT
## ```

weave is_mouse_button_pressed with button as raylib.MouseButton into bool:
    return calling raylib.IsMouseButtonPressed with (button to int)

## Reports whether a mouse button is currently held.
##
## ## Parameters
## - `button`: the button to test, as a `raylib.MouseButton`.
##
## ## Returns
## `true` when the condition holds for this frame, `false` otherwise.
##
## ## Memory
## No allocation; a single direct C call.
##
## ## Example
## ```pengu
## var down as bool is calling is_mouse_button_down with raylib.MOUSE_BUTTON_LEFT
## ```

weave is_mouse_button_down with button as raylib.MouseButton into bool:
    return calling raylib.IsMouseButtonDown with (button to int)

## Reports whether a mouse button went up during this frame.
##
## ## Parameters
## - `button`: the button to test, as a `raylib.MouseButton`.
##
## ## Returns
## `true` when the condition holds for this frame, `false` otherwise.
##
## ## Memory
## No allocation; a single direct C call.
##
## ## Example
## ```pengu
## var down as bool is calling is_mouse_button_released with raylib.MOUSE_BUTTON_LEFT
## ```

weave is_mouse_button_released with button as raylib.MouseButton into bool:
    return calling raylib.IsMouseButtonReleased with (button to int)

## Reports whether a mouse button is currently not held.
##
## ## Parameters
## - `button`: the button to test, as a `raylib.MouseButton`.
##
## ## Returns
## `true` when the condition holds for this frame, `false` otherwise.
##
## ## Memory
## No allocation; a single direct C call.
##
## ## Example
## ```pengu
## var down as bool is calling is_mouse_button_up with raylib.MOUSE_BUTTON_LEFT
## ```

weave is_mouse_button_up with button as raylib.MouseButton into bool:
    return calling raylib.IsMouseButtonUp with (button to int)

## Moves the mouse cursor to a position.
##
## ## Parameters
## - `position`: the target position in screen coordinates; the components are truncated to `int`.
##
## ## Returns
## Nothing. The move takes effect through the platform, so a subsequent read may lag by a frame.
##
## ## Memory
## No allocation; a single direct C call.
##
## ## Example
## ```pengu
## calling set_mouse_position with (with x is 100.0, y is 200.0)
## ```

weave set_mouse_position with position as raylib.Vector2 into void:
    calling raylib.SetMousePosition with (position.x to int), (position.y to int)

## Adds a fixed offset to every mouse position raylib reports.
##
## ## Parameters
## - `offset`: the offset to apply; the components are truncated to `int`.
##
## ## Returns
## Nothing. This is a global adjustment, useful for letterboxed or scaled render targets.
##
## ## Memory
## No allocation; a single direct C call.
##
## ## Example
## ```pengu
## calling set_mouse_offset with (with x is 0.0, y is 0.0)
## ```

weave set_mouse_offset with offset as raylib.Vector2 into void:
    calling raylib.SetMouseOffset with (offset.x to int), (offset.y to int)

## Scales every mouse position raylib reports.
##
## ## Parameters
## - `scale`: the factor to apply.
##
## ## Returns
## Nothing. Together with `set_mouse_offset` this maps window coordinates onto a virtual canvas.
##
## ## Memory
## No allocation; a single direct C call.
##
## ## Example
## ```pengu
## calling set_mouse_scale with (with x is 1.0, y is 1.0)
## ```

weave set_mouse_scale with scale as raylib.Vector2 into void:
    calling raylib.SetMouseScale with scale.x, scale.y

## Changes the mouse cursor shape.
##
## ## Parameters
## - `cursor`: the cursor to show, as a `raylib.MouseCursor`.
##
## ## Returns
## Nothing.
##
## ## Memory
## No allocation; a single direct C call.
##
## ## Example
## ```pengu
## calling set_mouse_cursor with raylib.MOUSE_CURSOR_CROSSHAIR
## ```

weave set_mouse_cursor with cursor as raylib.MouseCursor into void:
    calling raylib.SetMouseCursor with (cursor to int)

## Returns the touch identifier of a touch point.
##
## ## Parameters
## - `index`: the touch point slot to query.
##
## ## Returns
## `some` with the touch id, or `maybe none` when the index is out of range.
##
## ## Notes
## `rcore.c` returns `-1` for an index past `MAX_TOUCH_POINTS`, which is the predicate; an in-range slot that has never been touched reports id `0`, so `some 0` is a valid answer, not a failure.
##
## ## Memory
## No allocation; a single direct C call.
##
## ## Example
## ```pengu
## var id as maybe int is calling touch_point_id with 0
## ```

weave touch_point_id with index as int into maybe int:
    var id as int is calling raylib.GetTouchPointId with index
    if id < 0:
        return maybe none
    return some id

## Enables the given gesture recognisers, disabling every other one.
##
## ## Parameters
## - `gestures`: the gestures to enable; an empty slice disables all of them.
##
## ## Returns
## Nothing. The setting is global and replaces the previous mask rather than adding to it.
##
## ## Notes
## The flags are ORed with `|`, never with `or`: `or` is boolean-only and rejects `u32` (`E0005`).
## Inherited from the archived `rgesturesx`, an array literal cannot be passed in argument position, so the parameter is a slice.
##
## ## Memory
## No allocation; a single direct C call.
##
## ## Example
## ```pengu
## calling enable_gestures with (gestures at 0 to (gestures.length))
## ```

weave enable_gestures with gestures as slice of raylib.Gesture into void:
    var flags as u32 is 0
    var i as int is 0
    while i < (gestures.length):
        set flags is flags | ((gestures at i) to u32)
        set i is i + 1
    calling raylib.SetGesturesEnabled with flags

## Reports whether a specific gesture was detected this frame.
##
## ## Parameters
## - `gesture`: the gesture to test, as a `raylib.Gesture`.
##
## ## Returns
## `true` when that gesture is the one currently detected, `false` otherwise.
##
## ## Notes
## raylib recognises one gesture at a time, so this is a comparison against the current detection rather than a flag test.
##
## ## Memory
## No allocation; a single direct C call.
##
## ## Example
## ```pengu
## var tapped as bool is calling is_gesture_detected with raylib.GESTURE_TAP
## ```

weave is_gesture_detected with gesture as raylib.Gesture into bool:
    return calling raylib.IsGestureDetected with (gesture to u32)

## Returns the gesture currently being detected.
##
## ## Returns
## `some` with the gesture's ordinal, or `maybe none` when nothing is being detected.
##
## ## Notes
## raylib signals "nothing" with `GESTURE_NONE`, which is `0`.
## The value is an `int` rather than a `raylib.Gesture` because the compiler emits an invalid C cast when converting an `int` to a qualified omen (master plan D19); compare it as `g == (raylib.GESTURE_TAP to int)`.
##
## ## Memory
## No allocation; a single direct C call.
##
## ## Example
## ```pengu
## var g as maybe int is calling detected_gesture
## ```

weave detected_gesture into maybe int:
    var g as int is calling raylib.GetGestureDetected
    if g == 0:
        return maybe none
    return some g

# --- Tests ---

test "is_key_down and friends are false with no input":
    calling ward.assert_false with (calling is_key_down with raylib.KEY_SPACE)
    calling ward.assert_false with (calling is_key_pressed with raylib.KEY_SPACE)
    calling ward.assert_false with (calling is_key_pressed_repeat with raylib.KEY_SPACE)
    calling ward.assert_false with (calling is_key_released with raylib.KEY_SPACE)
    calling ward.assert_true with (calling is_key_up with raylib.KEY_A)

test "the keyboard predicates accept distinct keys":
    calling ward.assert_false with (calling is_key_down with raylib.KEY_ESCAPE)
    calling ward.assert_false with (calling is_key_down with raylib.KEY_ENTER)
    calling ward.assert_false with (calling is_key_down with raylib.KEY_LEFT_SHIFT)

test "key_pressed is none with an empty queue":
    var first as maybe int is calling key_pressed
    calling ward.assert_maybe_none with first
    var second as maybe int is calling key_pressed
    calling ward.assert_maybe_none with second

test "char_pressed is none with an empty queue":
    var first as maybe int is calling char_pressed
    calling ward.assert_maybe_none with first

test "key_name is a maybe whose value is never empty":
    # Measured: without a window this is `maybe none` (no keyboard layout); once GLFW has been
    # initialised it may be `some`. Asserting `none` unconditionally would make this test
    # depend on whether an earlier test opened a window, so it asserts the contract instead:
    # a `some` result carries a real, non-empty, owned string.
    var name as maybe string is calling key_name with raylib.KEY_A
    if name is present:
        var label as string is calling name.unwrap
        calling ward.assert_false with (label == "")

test "gamepad button predicates follow whether a device is available":
    # This depends on whether GLFW has enumerated a device, which depends on whether a window
    # has ever been created in the process -- so the test branches instead of assuming. Both
    # branches were measured with a real joystick present at /dev/input/js0:
    #   * before any window exists: `IsGamepadAvailable(0)` is false and all four are false;
    #   * once a window has been opened: the device is available, and `up` is the logical
    #     negation of `down` (true for an unpressed button).
    if calling raylib.IsGamepadAvailable with 0:
        calling ward.assert_false with (calling is_gamepad_button_down with 0, raylib.GAMEPAD_BUTTON_RIGHT_FACE_DOWN)
        calling ward.assert_true with (calling is_gamepad_button_up with 0, raylib.GAMEPAD_BUTTON_RIGHT_FACE_DOWN)
    else:
        calling ward.assert_false with (calling is_gamepad_button_down with 0, raylib.GAMEPAD_BUTTON_RIGHT_FACE_DOWN)
        calling ward.assert_false with (calling is_gamepad_button_pressed with 0, raylib.GAMEPAD_BUTTON_LEFT_FACE_UP)
        calling ward.assert_false with (calling is_gamepad_button_released with 0, raylib.GAMEPAD_BUTTON_MIDDLE_RIGHT)
        calling ward.assert_false with (calling is_gamepad_button_up with 0, raylib.GAMEPAD_BUTTON_RIGHT_TRIGGER_1)

test "gamepad_button_pressed is none with no gamepad":
    var b as maybe int is calling gamepad_button_pressed
    calling ward.assert_maybe_none with b

test "gamepad_axis_movement is zero only without a device":
    # Measured: with the real joystick at /dev/input/js0 enumerated, a resting axis is NOT
    # exactly zero (-0.0117647, about -3/255), which is precisely why dead zones exist and why
    # this test has to branch instead of demanding 0.0 unconditionally.
    if calling raylib.IsGamepadAvailable with 0:
        var x as f32 is calling gamepad_axis_movement with 0, raylib.GAMEPAD_AXIS_LEFT_X
        calling ward.assert_true with (x >= -1.0)
        calling ward.assert_true with (x <= 1.0)
    else:
        calling ward.assert_almost_eq with (calling gamepad_axis_movement with 0, raylib.GAMEPAD_AXIS_LEFT_X), 0.0, 0.001
        calling ward.assert_almost_eq with (calling gamepad_axis_movement with 0, raylib.GAMEPAD_AXIS_RIGHT_Y), 0.0, 0.001

test "gamepad_name is a string, empty only without a device":
    # Measured: with the real controller enumerated this returns its name
    # ("Sony Computer Entertainment Wireless Controller"), and an empty string otherwise.
    # Either way it is a plain `string`, never `maybe`: raylib returns "" rather than NULL.
    var who as string is calling gamepad_name with 0
    if calling raylib.IsGamepadAvailable with 0:
        calling ward.assert_false with (who == "")
    else:
        calling ward.assert_string_empty with who

test "set_gamepad_mappings never returns a negative count":
    calling ward.assert_ge_int with (calling set_gamepad_mappings with ""), 0
    calling ward.assert_ge_int with (calling set_gamepad_mappings with "not a mapping"), 0

test "mouse button predicates are false with no input":
    calling ward.assert_false with (calling is_mouse_button_down with raylib.MOUSE_BUTTON_LEFT)
    calling ward.assert_false with (calling is_mouse_button_pressed with raylib.MOUSE_BUTTON_RIGHT)
    calling ward.assert_false with (calling is_mouse_button_released with raylib.MOUSE_BUTTON_MIDDLE)
    calling ward.assert_true with (calling is_mouse_button_up with raylib.MOUSE_BUTTON_SIDE)

test "the mouse setters are callable headless":
    calling set_mouse_position with (with x is 100.0, y is 200.0)
    calling set_mouse_offset with (with x is 0.0, y is 0.0)
    calling set_mouse_scale with (with x is 1.0, y is 1.0)
    calling set_mouse_cursor with raylib.MOUSE_CURSOR_CROSSHAIR
    var pos as raylib.Vector2 is calling raylib.GetMousePosition
    calling ward.assert_false with (pos.x != pos.x)

test "touch_point_id distinguishes invalid slots from id zero":
    var invalid as maybe int is calling touch_point_id with 1000
    calling ward.assert_maybe_none with invalid
    var first as maybe int is calling touch_point_id with 0
    calling ward.assert_maybe_present with first
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with (calling first.unwrap), 0

test "enable_gestures accepts an empty slice as a no-op":
    var none as list of raylib.Gesture is list of raylib.Gesture
    defer banish none
    calling enable_gestures with (none at 0 to 0)
    calling ward.assert_false with (calling is_gesture_detected with raylib.GESTURE_TAP)

test "enable_gestures combines several flags":
    var flags as list of raylib.Gesture is list of raylib.Gesture
    defer banish flags
    calling flags.push with raylib.GESTURE_TAP
    calling flags.push with raylib.GESTURE_DRAG
    calling enable_gestures with (flags at 0 to (flags.length))
    calling ward.assert_false with (calling is_gesture_detected with raylib.GESTURE_DRAG)
    calling ward.assert_false with (calling is_gesture_detected with raylib.GESTURE_PINCH_IN)

test "detected_gesture is none when nothing is happening":
    var g as maybe int is calling detected_gesture
    calling ward.assert_maybe_none with g

# Every wrapped entry is referenced here so that no signature can rot unnoticed. The
# probe is not invoked: the family is already asserted by the tests above, and this one
# exists only for the type checker.
weave input_api_probe into void:
    var a as bool is calling is_key_pressed with raylib.KEY_A
    var b as bool is calling is_key_pressed_repeat with raylib.KEY_A
    var c as bool is calling is_key_down with raylib.KEY_A
    var d as bool is calling is_key_released with raylib.KEY_A
    var e as bool is calling is_key_up with raylib.KEY_A
    var f as maybe int is calling key_pressed
    var g as maybe int is calling char_pressed
    var h as maybe string is calling key_name with raylib.KEY_A
    var i as bool is calling is_gamepad_button_pressed with 0, raylib.GAMEPAD_BUTTON_LEFT_FACE_UP
    var j as bool is calling is_gamepad_button_down with 0, raylib.GAMEPAD_BUTTON_LEFT_FACE_UP
    var k as bool is calling is_gamepad_button_released with 0, raylib.GAMEPAD_BUTTON_LEFT_FACE_UP
    var l as bool is calling is_gamepad_button_up with 0, raylib.GAMEPAD_BUTTON_LEFT_FACE_UP
    var m as maybe int is calling gamepad_button_pressed
    var n as f32 is calling gamepad_axis_movement with 0, raylib.GAMEPAD_AXIS_LEFT_X
    var o as string is calling gamepad_name with 0
    var p as int is calling set_gamepad_mappings with "x"
    var q as bool is calling is_mouse_button_pressed with raylib.MOUSE_BUTTON_LEFT
    var r as bool is calling is_mouse_button_down with raylib.MOUSE_BUTTON_LEFT
    var s as bool is calling is_mouse_button_released with raylib.MOUSE_BUTTON_LEFT
    var t as bool is calling is_mouse_button_up with raylib.MOUSE_BUTTON_LEFT
    calling set_mouse_position with (with x is 0.0, y is 0.0)
    calling set_mouse_offset with (with x is 0.0, y is 0.0)
    calling set_mouse_scale with (with x is 1.0, y is 1.0)
    calling set_mouse_cursor with raylib.MOUSE_CURSOR_DEFAULT
    var u as maybe int is calling touch_point_id with 0
    var flags as list of raylib.Gesture is list of raylib.Gesture
    defer banish flags
    calling enable_gestures with (flags at 0 to 0)
    var v as bool is calling is_gesture_detected with raylib.GESTURE_TAP
    var w as maybe int is calling detected_gesture
